*Executive summary.* When a tube preamplifier, studio console or vintage hi-fi unit fails, a technician must find which of its many parts failed, and specialist shops charge for that bench evaluation. Differential simulates how every cataloged part failure changes a modeled circuit's readings (@fig:concept), keeps a probability for every group of suspects that readings cannot tell apart, and recommends the measurement expected to narrow them most per unit of effort; a language model reads the complaint and explains; the technician takes every reading and safety record. In a simulated pilot it named the failed part's group first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged units, with << num(bt.T9.estimate, 2) >> times the effort of a scripted half-split search using the same inference; no hardware result exists yet. The locked primary targets (@tab:targets) include << num(T['T1'].pass_count, 0) >> of << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> simulated units right, << num(T['T6'].pass_count, 0) >> of << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged and << T['T7'].pass_count >> of << ev.fault_board_min >> faults on real 24 V boards; each will be reported met or missed.

