*Interviews before M2*, after the Stevens IRB's determination: six 45-minute interviews (two technicians from independent audio shops, a studio maintenance engineer, a trainee, a shop owner, a vintage-gear owner), recruited through audio societies and trade contacts, never an employer, on diagnosis time, recurring faults, judging readings, what a tool may do and consent to removals; and 50 tickets from one shop, stripped of customer and technician identities by the shop before transfer, with each technician's consent sought privately, coded for faults and time to find them, reported pooled.

#decision("The primary user is a qualified bench technician")[
*Options:* a consumer self-repair assistant or a professional bench tool. *Choice:* professional: diagnosis often means testing a powered, open chassis, and << hv.hv_normal + hv.hv_under_fault >> of the channel strip's << cs.test_points >> test points can exceed 50 V (@tab:library). OSHA requires employers to de-energize live parts unless testing needs power (narrow exceptions below 50 V) and to let only qualified persons work on or test them << cite('osha_1910_333', 'osha_1910_331_399', 'osha_1910_334') >>; the self-employed fall outside the OSH Act << cite('osh_act_1970_sec3_5_employer', 'ecfr_29_1904_31') >>, so our terms of use require it. *Consequence:* offered to repair businesses, trainees supervised, mains-side work refused; hobbyists keep forums and chat assistants.
]

<< part(2, "How diagnosis is done today, and the data landscape") >>

*Today's practice* (the interviews will check it): with a multimeter, an oscilloscope, a signal generator and a variac or series lamp << cite('goldwasser_audio_repair_notes_1996', 'esp_troubleshooting_repair_guide') >>, a technician confirms the complaint; looks for burnt or bulging parts; checks the supplies; follows a test tone stage by stage, often halving the search ("half-split tracing"); compares readings with the manual's voltage chart or the healthy channel << cite('esp_p164_signal_tracer') >>; tests tubes and electrolytics (tube tester, ESR meter); unsolders a suspect and confirms the fault clears. In our reading, the time goes to judging which reading is really "off", since parts sit anywhere within tolerance and aging electrolytics lose capacitance and gain series resistance << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, and to unsoldering suspects that prove good; the ticket coding (Part 1) will measure it. Replacing every electrolytic ("recapping") often cures a symptom but costs parts and time, risks the board and never names the failed part.

*The information is uneven.* Manuals with schematics and voltage charts exist for many classic models, but units are often modified or undocumented. U.S. repair laws cover newer digital products, not vintage analog gear: New York's took effect in December 2023 << cite('ny_gbl_399nn', 'ny_ag_repair_2024') >>, and six other states followed with limits by date or product << cite('ca_prc_42488', 'co_hb24_1121', 'mn_325e72_digital_fair_repair', 'or_sb1596_2024', 'wa_eshb1483_2025', 'tx_hb2963_2025') >>; the EU's obligation to repair covers only its Annex II products, such as washing machines << cite('eu_r2r_directive_2024_1799', 'ec_r2r_qa_2024') >>. So Differential needs a model per product, built from the schematic, and trusts its hazard map (the points that can exceed 50 V) only once the model's healthy readings match the manual's chart; until then every point of a high-voltage unit counts as high voltage. "No single fault fits" covers modified units; units without a schematic are out of scope.

*Labeled fault data is scarce, so we simulate it.* Confirming a failed part takes unsoldering and is rarely recorded; shop logs are private and, after recapping, unlabeled. We know of no public dataset of bench readings with confirmed failed parts; published methods use benchmark filters << cite('gao_yang_jiang_2021_incipient_tim') >> and simulated data << cite('cloete_stander_wilke_2022_access') >>. Analog-test research built simulated fault dictionaries << cite('bandler_salama_1985') >>, found faults the test points cannot tell apart (ambiguity groups) << cite('stenbakken_souders_stewart_1989') >> and applied machine learning << cite('he_he_li_2020_gan_tim') >>; we follow it (@tab:library). Other label sources fall short: faults built into real boards give true labels but each is built and measured by hand, so they test transfer only; expert fault trees are one per product; forum threads and service bulletins report fixes that worked, without readings. A simulated unit draws every part within its tolerance, the mains within ±<< m('library.instrument.mains_tol_pct') | round | int >>% (our assumption), and one cataloged fault (an open, short, drift, or a mode such as a leaky transistor or worn tube). Since units reaching a bench are old, << design.simulation.aging.training_share_pct | round | int >>% of training units are also aged: electrolytics up to << m('library.aging.electrolytic_c_loss_max_pct') | round | int >>% low with up to << m('library.aging.electrolytic_esr_max_x') | round | int >>× the series resistance (past the usual end-of-life limits of 20% and 2× << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>), resistors up to << m('library.aging.resistor_drift_max_pct') | round | int >>% high, tubes up to << m('library.aging.triode_emission_loss_max_pct') | round | int >>% weaker (our assumptions). Each reading carries instrument error: a << m('library.instrument.dmm_input_mohm') | round | int >> MΩ meter loads each DC node << cite('fluke_11x_extended_specs') >> and reads within ±(<< num(m('library.instrument.dmm_pct'), 1) >>% + << m('library.instrument.dmm_counts') >> counts) << cite('fluke_117_technical_data') >>, an oscilloscope within ±<< num(m('library.instrument.scope_pct'), 0) >>% of full scale << cite('rigol_ds1000z_user_guide_ch18') >>. The library holds << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> units (<< m('library.simulations.failed_draws') >> failed to converge). Hand calculations reproduce all << m('hand_calcs.rows_checked') >> healthy bias points and gains checked, each within a tolerance set beforehand. For faults, an audit re-solved << fa.faults >> randomly drawn faults (up to two per fault type and circuit) with a solver we wrote from the published device equations, without the simulator: all << fa.faults_agree >> agreed within twice the meter's accuracy (95% interval << pct(fa.ci95[0], 0) >>-100%): the audit solver was corrected on the first 42, where 6 had disagreed through its own omissions, and the channel strip's << fa.faults - 42 >>, drawn afterwards, agreed at first comparison. That checks the simulator, not reality; the real fault boards (T7, Part 3) test reality. A complaint alone is weak: on the channel strip a hum complaint fits << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.faults_per_symptom.hum') >> faults. Labels are scarce, readings ambiguous and complaints weak: a tool must learn from simulation and choose its readings.

