*How it will be measured.* _Test sets:_ << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> single-fault units (<< num(ev.channel_strip_cases, 0) >> channel strip, << ev.cases_per_block >> per block) and << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged units with the same mix, on seeds disjoint from training (aging within the training ranges); faults are drawn uniformly and kept only on units that show a symptom, not by field failure rates. _Complaints:_ from a phrase bank the complaint reader (rules offline, the language model live) was not developed on (its overall score was seen once, before rules were extended from development text only; T18 uses a sealed bank), in three voices (a bench technician, a hobbyist owner, a studio client), never naming a part, each symptom dropped with probability << m('library.complaint_noise.p_omit_pct') | round | int >>% and others added with << m('library.complaint_noise.p_add_pct') | round | int >>%. _Stress sets:_ << num(ev.unmodeled_cases, 0) >> units outside the catalog (double faults, wrong-value parts, solder bridges) and tolerances 1.5-3 times wider (a curve). _Baselines:_ a fixed-order service chart, half-split tracing (halving the signal path; the strongest script) and random probing (never unsolders) share the engine's inference and stopping rule; the scripts unsolder their leading suspect at << pct(design.engine.scripts_confirm_at_by.fixed_order, 0) >> (chart) or << pct(design.engine.scripts_confirm_at_by.half_split, 0) >> (half-split) belief, each tuned for them on the pilot; a language model, alone and with our simulator as a tool, picks from the same list on << ev.llm_subset >> stratified units and stops when it names an answer; a healthy-channel comparison, usual in stereo gear, joins them at M2 without a bar (it needs twin units on a shared supply). _Real boards (T7):_ a teammate who does not record draws << ev.fault_board_min >> buildable faults at random and inserts them in sealed order into three 24 V line-driver boards, one built from used parts; each point is recorded once per fault, every method is replayed on the recordings, and results are reported per board and beside simulation on the same faults. _User study S1:_ six or more technicians timed with and without the tool, without a bar (six cannot show a benefit reliably); its planted-error rule is gate T25.

*How the bars were set; no test unit has been run.* A separate pilot (<< m('pilot2.n_total') >> units as-new and << m('pilot2.aged_n') >> aged, weighted like the test set; << m('bars.targets.T13.n_pilot') | int >> out-of-catalog) gave: right group first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged units, a << num(((100 * bt.T1.estimate) | round(1)) - ((100 * bt.T6.estimate) | round(1)), 1) >>-point cost of aging (of aged units, << pct(bt.T6.aged_flag_rate, 1) >> flagged, << pct(bt.T6.aged_wrong_name_rate, 1) >> misnamed). <% set ep = eff.engine.probing | round(1) %><% set eu = (eff.engine.unsoldering + eff.engine.confirming_charge) | round(1) %><% set hp = eff.half_split.probing | round(1) %><% set hu = (eff.half_split.unsoldering + eff.half_split.confirming_charge) | round(1) %>To a confirmed answer, the engine alone spent << num(ep + eu, 1) >> effort units and half-split tracing << num(hp + hu, 1) >>; the engine asked for << num(m('bars.context.hv_steps.engine'), 1) >> powered readings per diagnosis at points that can exceed 50 V, <% set h1 = num(m('bars.context.hv_steps.half_split'), 1) %><% set h2 = num(m('bars.context.hv_steps.fixed_order'), 1) %>the scripts << h1 if h1 == h2 else h1 ~ "-" ~ h2 >> (T24). <% set pc = targets.targets | selectattr('primary') | selectattr('chance_met', 'defined') | map(attribute='chance_met') | list %>Each bar is the pilot estimate moved << m('bars.k') >> standard errors against the system (T1 << num(100 * bt.T1.se, 1) >>, T6 << num(100 * bt.T6.se, 1) >>, T13 << num(100 * bt.T13.se, 1) >> points) and rounded, never past the estimate, with one bar per part (T4); counting the pilot's own error, each primary is met with a chance of << (100 * (pc | min)) | round | int >>-<< (100 * (pc | max)) | round | int >>%. T5 has no pilot (no API key): +10 points is the smallest margin we judged worth a per-circuit model. T7's 70% is what << ev.fault_board_min >> faults can show: a pass needs << T['T7'].pass_count >> right, which a system truly 90% accurate reaches << pct(T['T7'].pass_probability['0.90'], 0) >> of the time, an 85% one << pct(T['T7'].pass_probability['0.85'], 0) >> and a 70% one << pct(T['T7'].pass_probability['0.70'], 0) >>, so a pass allows about a 10-point drop from simulation. Each primary is judged alone; if independent, all << words[pc | length] >> pilot-estimable primaries would pass together with a chance near << (100 * targets.joint_chance_primaries.chance) | round | int >>%, about << words[targets.joint_chance_primaries.expected_misses | round | int] >> missing.

*Scope.* In: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the symptoms they cause (no or low output, hum, distortion, bass or treble loss, output DC) in supplies, preamplifier and line-level stages. Out: power amplifiers; hiss, intermittent, thermal and mechanical faults; double faults (flagged); unpowered checks and visual inspection; consumer self-repair; mains-side work; unattended probing. *Plan (owner):* interviews and ticket coding by M2, week 8 (Lead 1); by M3, week 11, the boards, T7 and one product's low-voltage section transcribed and checked against its manual (Lead 2) and the full evaluation (Lead 3); S1 and live runs by M5, week 13 (Lead 4). Late results stay pending, never replaced.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and effort to a confirmed answer")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or group accuracy and weighted effort to a confirmed answer. *Choice:* the latter: per-part scoring rewards lucky guesses between faults with identical readings, and an answer is worth acting on only once confirmed. *Consequence:* group sizes and raw effort are reported too.
]

<< part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics") >>

*Why a hybrid.* Language models guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing << cite('kalai_why_hallucinate_2025') >>. Alone, a model has no expected readings for this unit under tolerance, no calibrated belief over hundreds of suspects and no cost per measurement; with a simulator as a tool << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >> it checks one guess at a time but lacks the last two (T5 and a model-with-simulator baseline test this). Simulation supplies the physics; Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect group, updated after each reading through likelihoods learned from simulated units: one to three full-covariance Gaussians per fault (chosen by the Bayesian information criterion) over the circuit's readings, so any subset of readings has a closed-form likelihood. "No single fault fits" is a hypothesis with a broad flat density, set on as-new validation units to flag at most << pct(m('library.design.false_alarm_budget'), 0) >> of single faults. Suspects start equal (the field's fault mix is unknown); the complaint's symptoms reweight them through a symptom model learned from simulation; T14 tests a wrong prior. Boosted trees << cite('ke_2017_lightgbm') >> are a comparison engine (as-new pilot top-1 without the complaint: << pct(m('bars.context.engine_disc_top1_weighted'), 1) >> against << pct(m('bars.context.accuracy_weighted.engine'), 1) >>). The next measurement maximizes expected information gain per unit of effort, the one-step entropy rule of model-based diagnosis << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> with a cost term (an optimal sequence needs a search << cite('pattipati_alexandridis_1990') >>); a diagnosis stops at << pct(m('library.design.stop_threshold'), 0) >> belief in one group, at the budget, or when nothing informative remains. The language model (Claude, version pinned) reads complaints (offline, rules do), proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation (offline, image processing) and explains; every number it writes must trace to a reading (T17), and it stays only if it beats a model alone (T5) and reads complaints and photos well (T18, T19).

*Set aside:* consistency-based candidates << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> (tolerances blur conflicts); retrieval over manuals (they describe healthy units and common faults); deep networks that condition on any subset of readings << cite('ivanov_vaeac_2019', 'li_acflow_2020') >> and choose measurements by information gain << cite('ma_eddi_2019') >>, but harder to audit than one density per fault. *Autonomy:* on five levels adapted from << cite('parasuraman_sheridan_wickens_2000') >> (L0, none, to L4, unattended), Differential is L2: it ranks suspects and recommends steps; a person takes every reading.

*Safety.* The hazard map covers healthy and faulted units, and a fault can put high voltage where there is normally none: channel-strip point << hv.showcase.tp >>, normally about << num(hv.showcase.normal_max_v, 0) >> V, reaches << num(hv.showcase.worst_case_v, 0) >> V under "<< hv.showcase.fault_label >>". Steps at points above 50 V carry hands-off and discharge warnings; in a high-voltage chassis every powered step says "high voltage inside", names a meter rated << design.safety.meter_rating | replace(" (IEC 61010)", "") >> and waits until the technician records in a form how the unit was brought up (variac, series lamp, current-limited supply, or already running). Before a part is removed, the owner's approval and a reading under << num(m('library.design.discharge_verify_max_v'), 0) >> V at every point that can hold high voltage are recorded; the check lapses after << design.safety.discharge_valid_min | round | int >> minutes unless a bleeder is clipped across the main filter capacitor, as a discharged capacitor recovers charge << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, and on any power-up. These are checks, not guarantees: the locks act on the technician's form entries, so they bound what the tool recommends and records, not what a person does; the residual shock risk rests on the user's unchecked qualification, and the hazard map covers only the modeled circuit. It cannot yet recommend the unpowered checks bench practice starts with (resistance, diode, ESR).

*People.* Runner-up suspects and reasons are shown, yet AI can mislead: consultants given GPT-4 were 19 percentage points less likely to solve a task outside its competence << cite('dellacqua_jagged_frontier_2023') >>, and those trusting AI more report less critical thinking << cite('lee_critical_thinking_2025') >>. Trainees commit their own next step before seeing the tool's and need a named supervisor near high voltage; a tutor giving hints instead of answers largely mitigated the learning loss an unrestricted chat model caused << cite('bastani_genai_learning_2025') >>. S1 plants one wrong recommendation on a non-hazardous step of a 24 V board, after the IRB's determination; participants are told beforehand that some advice may be wrong, are debriefed and recruited outside their employers (T25). Whether owners turn faster diagnosis into a faster pace is a question for the interviews. As fairness audits do for groups of people << cite('buolamwini_gebru_2018') >>, errors are broken down by complaint voice, since owners who describe faults in plain words could get worse diagnoses (non-English complaints are not covered; breakdowns by fault type show testability, not fairness): a voice more than 5 points worse on the T1 or T6 units, with an interval excluding zero, blocks release (T26).

*Accountability.* The team answers for the hazard map, stated performance and corrections; a shop, for its circuit models and staff; the technician, for measurements and precautions. Responsibility should follow control, lest the technician become a moral crumple zone, blamed for a system they cannot steer << cite('elish_2019_moral_crumple_zones') >>: the repair ticket records the tool's version, what it recommended and why, and what the technician entered in the forms; it stays in the shop's system, and the terms of use forbid using it to rate, discipline or pay staff. Of the rules in Part 1, OSHA's is met by selling to businesses and supervising trainees, the AI Act's by telling owners of the AI and forbidding staff rating, and the FTC's by quoting only locked results, labeled simulated or real. Whether advice software is a product (strict liability) or a service (negligence) is unsettled in the U.S.: bills would make AI systems products << cite('us_ai_lead_act_s2937', 'us_s2937_ai_lead_act_billstatus') >> or shield developers whose tools licensed professionals use, which bench technicians are not << cite('us_rise_act_s2081') >>; the revised EU directive makes software placed on the market after 9 December 2026 a product << cite('eu_pld_2024_2853') >>, and California bars defending a harm claim by saying the AI acted autonomously << cite('ca_ab316_2025_chaptered') >>.

